\vskip 18pt

\section {Introduction}

This document is typeset more than once. On the first pass the engine learns
where every numbered heading lands; on the next it fills in the table of contents
above and the references below. Forward references work because of it: the
conclusion is section~\ref{sec:conclusion}, which begins on page~\pageref{sec:conclusion},
and the worked example is section~\ref{sec:example} on page~\pageref{sec:example}.

\label{sec:intro}A reference can also point backward, of course --- this
introduction is section~\ref{sec:intro}.

\section {Background}

\label{sec:background}A cross-reference binds a name to a numbered thing with \texttt{label}, then names
it later with \texttt{ref} (the number) or \texttt{pageref} (the page). The number
is captured where the label is written; the page is learned when that page ships,
so neither has to be known in advance.

\subsection {How the table of contents is built}

Each sectioning command quietly records its level, number, title and page. The
\texttt{tableofcontents} command replays those entries, drawing a dotted leader
from each title to its page number --- the classic use of leaders.

\vfill\eject

\section {A worked example}

\label{sec:example}This section sits on its own page so its number and folio are
worth referencing from the introduction. Look back at the contents: this is
section~\ref{sec:example}, and the background discussion was
section~\ref{sec:background}.

\vfill\eject

\section {Conclusion}

\label{sec:conclusion}Forward and backward references, and a table of contents
with real page numbers, all fall out of typesetting the document twice over one
shared table. This conclusion is section~\ref{sec:conclusion} on
page~\pageref{sec:conclusion}.

\vfill\eject
